WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.877
Socket clients should control where they connect.

2
00:00:02.877 --> 00:00:11.843
A configured endpoint, approved service record, or allowlisted destination
is usually safer than accepting arbitrary hosts and ports from untrusted
input.

3
00:00:11.843 --> 00:00:19.270
User-supplied destinations can send a server-side client toward unintended
public, private, internal, or sensitive locations.

4
00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:28.035
DNS and name resolution behavior may change over time, so destination
policy should be expressed at the application level rather than left to
convenience.

5
00:00:28.035 --> 00:00:32.117
Connection timeouts, read timeouts, and write timeouts are essential.

6
00:00:32.117 --> 00:00:39.343
A client that waits indefinitely can tie up workers, command-line jobs,
automation tasks, or background processing capacity.

7
00:00:39.343 --> 00:00:43.558
A slow or unavailable peer should not be able to consume resources
forever.

8
00:00:43.558 --> 00:00:52.323
Timeouts should match the business purpose of the operation and should
produce safe, observable failure behavior rather than ambiguous partial
success.

9
00:00:52.323 --> 00:00:54.264
Retries and backoff require care.

10
00:00:54.264 --> 00:01:05.705
Retrying a connection may help with transient network issues, but repeated
attempts can amplify outages, overload a dependency, or duplicate state-
changing work if the protocol is not designed for it.

11
00:01:05.705 --> 00:01:12.931
A client should know whether an operation is safe to retry, how many
attempts are reasonable, and how failure should be reported.

12
00:01:12.931 --> 00:01:17.481
Retry behavior is part of the protocol contract, not just a reliability
setting.

13
00:01:17.481 --> 00:01:26.246
Client logging should avoid exposing credentials, connection strings,
tokens, private addresses where sensitive, raw payloads, and protocol
secrets.

14
00:01:26.246 --> 00:01:34.142
Logs can still record safe destination identifiers, operation names,
connection outcomes, timeouts, retry counts, and correlation IDs.

15
00:01:34.142 --> 00:01:42.840
A secure client validates where it connects, how long it waits, what it
sends, what it accepts from the peer, and how failures become operational
evidence.
